WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.399
What's a good horror ending?

00:00:02.399 --> 00:00:06.780
It's scary, sure, but it can also be funny,
emotionally wrought, and so mysterious that

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.780
it raises more questions than it answers.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:12.809
Put simply, the best horror film endings are
the ones that stay with you long after you've

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:13.800
left the theater.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.680
So much of Rosemary's Baby takes the form
of a paranoia-driven thriller that, from a

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:22.490
certain perspective, you could almost frame
it as the story of a pregnant woman who has

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:26.980
a strange dream one night and then grows increasingly
terrified of the people around her.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:31.130
It could be that they're only trying to help,
as some kind of hormonal imbalance or mental

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.660
health issue drives her to think they're all
out to get her.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.390
That's how much of the film plays, and it's
easy to see how other filmmakers might have

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:39.090
leaned into that even more.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:43.329
But Roman Polanski's film all builds to that
ending, in which Rosemary discovers that all

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:48.060
of her paranoia has been engineered by a coven
of Satanists, who arranged for the Devil himself

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:51.220
to impregnate her, and that she has given
birth to the Antichrist.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.670
The coven's calm celebration as they stand
around the bassinet — not to mention the

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:59.230
film's refusal to actually show us a clear
shot of the child — only adds to the dread,

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:03.080
which culminates in Rosemary accepting that
she could indeed perhaps be a mother to this

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:04.080
child.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.499
"What have you done to it?

00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:09.920
What have you done to its eyes?"

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.600
"He has his father's eyes."

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.160
The Wicker Man is one of the strangest horror
movies in the history of the genre.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:20.130
"Oh, no, not the bees!

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.979
NOT THE BEES — AHHHHH.

00:01:21.979 --> 00:01:24.299
AHH, THEY'RE IN MY EYES.

00:01:24.299 --> 00:01:25.299
MY EYES!”

00:01:25.299 --> 00:01:30.170
No, not THAT Wicker Man… we’re talking,
of course, about the original 1973 film upon

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:32.899
which the Nicolas Cage-led remake was based.

00:01:32.899 --> 00:01:37.100
The original Wicker Man is a film that plays
out in many ways like some kind of dream,

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:40.729
because most of its characters seem to be
living in an entirely different reality than

00:01:40.729 --> 00:01:42.280
the protagonist, Sgt. Howie.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.560
They dance and smile and walk dreamily through
life as though nothing is wrong, despite the

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.860
fact that Howie has come to investigate the
alleged disappearance of one of their own

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:50.860
children.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:54.470
As the film moves forward, we get the impression
that he's about to shatter all of their illusions.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:57.939
Then, in the film's final minutes, they instead
shatter his.

00:01:57.939 --> 00:02:01.899
The ending of this film is easily and instantly
compelling, thanks in part to the striking

00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:05.179
visual of the titular wicker man burning against
the setting sun.

00:02:05.179 --> 00:02:08.890
It's a haunting image, one that works in the
film's favor even now.

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:12.470
What really makes the ending work, though,
is Howie screaming out Bible verses as he's

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:16.819
burning alive while the cultists around him
sing their own song of Pagan celebration.

00:02:16.819 --> 00:02:20.770
It's not the ending you predict as Howie seems
to get closer and closer to the truth.

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:23.599
Even though he's a bit of a stick in the mud,
you're still rooting for him to thwart the

00:02:23.599 --> 00:02:25.870
human sacrifices happening on the island.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:30.120
Then, as the wicker man burns, you're left
feeling both horrified and spellbound.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.280
1974's Black Christmas is one of the most
important precursors to the modern slasher

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:37.480
film genre for a number of reasons.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:41.319
It's got the point-of-view cinematography,
the slow ratcheting up of the body count,

00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:45.970
the holiday setting, the sorority house, and
it even has the final girl in the form of

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:47.780
Olivia Hussey's Jess.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:52.129
Where the film departs from many other final
girl narratives is in its haunting and horrifying

00:02:52.129 --> 00:02:56.340
closing scene, in which Jess is left alone
to rest in her bedroom while police officers

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:57.930
guard the house from outside.

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.849
The camera then reveals that no one has bothered
to check the attic, where two bodies and the

00:03:01.849 --> 00:03:06.569
real killer are still waiting for their moment,
ensuring that Jess is still in terrible danger.

00:03:06.569 --> 00:03:11.040
The cheerful Christmas lights and snowy landscape
juxtaposed with that persistent ringing phone

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.170
as the credits begin to roll are among the
most chill-inducing things in the history

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:16.440
of slasher cinema.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.989
It's common practice in horror cinema to lull
the viewer into a false sense of finality

00:03:20.989 --> 00:03:25.760
before unleashing an ending that suddenly
and terrifyingly swerves for one last jump

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:26.760
scare.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:30.319
It was done famously in Friday the 13th and
A Nightmare on Elm Street, as well as countless

00:03:30.319 --> 00:03:32.040
other films of the 1980s.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:36.120
But even before those films established that
One Last Scare philosophy, there was Brian

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.010
De Palma's Carrie, and its final freaky moments.

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:43.310
When Sue Snell visits the site of Carrie White's
burned-down home, we are immediately aware

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:47.140
that something is off thanks to De Palma's
dreamlike construction of the scene.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:50.530
Still, everything seems peaceful, concluded,
and calm.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:51.609
Then, well….

00:03:51.609 --> 00:03:52.760
This crap goes down:

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:59.000
[Screaming]

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.260
Yikes.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:03.019
Even though the film makes it clear that we
are indeed watching a nightmare, the fear

00:04:03.019 --> 00:04:04.189
is still real.

00:04:04.189 --> 00:04:08.069
It's real because of the masterful way the
sequence is directed, but also because after

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.560
all we've seen Carrie do, coming back from
the dead doesn't exactly seem so far-fetched.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:18.090
[Tires squealing]

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.920
[Explosion]

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:25.160
The Shining is a film so pregnant with symbolism,
mystique, and meaning for fans nearly four

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:29.860
decades after its release that entire documentaries
have been devoted to decoding its many supposed

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:30.980
hidden messages.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:35.220
It's so dense with possible interpretations,
and nowhere is that clearer than its ending.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:39.310
When all the chaos at the Overlook Hotel has
died down, the camera zooms in on a 60-year-old

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:43.660
photo to reveal the smiling face of Jack Torrance,
and everyone leaves the theater or turns off

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:45.380
the TV scratching their heads.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:49.160
Is Jack a reincarnation of another man who
came to the Overlook decades ago?

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:52.690
Is he simply another version of the same spirit
who keeps returning to this ground?

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:55.920
Is the Overlook perpetuating a cycle of violence
to sustain itself?

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:00.310
Never has being so uncertain about a horror
film's ending been quite so intellectually

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:05.040
satisfying, and we've got nearly 40 years
of theories, essays, and films to prove it.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.120
"They're here!"

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.000
Poltergeist is one of those films that could
have easily front-loaded all of its good ideas

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:15.200
into the meaty scares for the first and second
acts of the story, leaving the finale a little

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.600
too predictable and a little too stiff.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:21.840
After all, the scene of young Carol Ann ominously
announcing the arrival of spectral visitors

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.200
through the TV is iconic in itself, and many
ghost movies might have just coasted on that

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:27.850
one striking image.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:30.780
Thankfully for all of us, that's not what
Poltergeist does.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:34.350
We can talk about the clown doll, the pool
full of bodies, the creatures from beyond,

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:37.720
and everything else that comes up during the
final confrontation in the film, but what

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:42.320
really makes it all work is the house's eventual
collapse into an extradimensional ball of

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:43.320
debris.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:47.380
In that moment, Poltergeist goes from instant
classic to eternal classic, the kind of film

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:48.490
we'll talk about forever.

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:53.210
Then, to add even more brilliance to the crazy
finale, we get that TV getting pushed out

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:54.720
to the hotel patio.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.410
Some horror films leave you with one last
scare, but this film leaves you with one last

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:01.160
laugh as the whole audience nods in agreement.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:10.780
Francis Ford Coppola's adaptation of Dracula
was an attempt to bring Bram Stoker's original

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:15.380
novel to the screen in a way that both utilized
lush practical effects in a memorable way

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:19.210
and also paid tribute to the book's themes
and operatic sense of storytelling.

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:23.710
The result is one of the most vivid horror
films of the 1990s, and its ending is nothing

00:06:23.710 --> 00:06:25.060
short of beautiful.

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:29.430
After finally reclaiming his reincarnated
bride in the form of Mina, Dracula attempts

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:33.340
to take her back home to Transylvania, only
to ultimately be defeated.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:36.930
Instead of simply dispatching the monster,
though, Coppola's film lingers on the love

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:41.840
story, allowing things to culminate in a heartbreaking
moment in which Mina sees the tragedy of Dracula

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:45.310
as an eternal man longing for a reunion with
the love of his life.

00:06:45.310 --> 00:06:49.270
Its final moments offer some consolation,
as we see that Dracula and his bride seem

00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:53.060
to have finally reconnected in the afterlife
after centuries apart.

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:55.840
Also Dracula's head gets cut off, which is
pretty rad.

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:07.770
Yeah, sure, The Cabin in the Woods might be
all about spoofing horror movie tropes, but

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:12.200
its commentary doesn't land unless the film
is also able to deliver on the horror goods.

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:16.310
You can't make a convincing meta-satire unless
you've also got the real elements on which

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:20.500
the satire is based, and it's there that The
Cabin in the Woods really starts to excel.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:26.990
"They made us choose… they made us choose
how we die."

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:31.100
This is particularly true of the film's climax,
in which all of the horrors of the underground

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:33.160
facility are unleashed at once.

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:37.280
It's a wonderfully wicked way to both show
us something scary and also make fun of horror

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:40.970
movie expectations, but then the film goes
a step further with the revelation that this

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:43.530
is all a ritual designed to preserve humanity.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:47.730
The film's final moment, in which the surviving
heroes decide to let the human race be destroyed,

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:51.280
is both a subversion of what we expect to
happen and a beautifully engineered final

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:52.320
jolt of horror.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:57.000
For much of its runtime, The Witch seems to
be the story of a devout religious family

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:01.060
who begins to see hexes and demonic influences
all around them when their baby is taken by

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:02.060
a wolf.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:05.690
The isolation the Puritan family faces, plus
their sense that everything they do is either

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:10.310
God's will or the result of unclean behaviors,
could naturally produce such a paranoid reaction

00:08:10.310 --> 00:08:11.310
to a tragedy.

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:15.060
The Witch could have played the whole film
that way, with no explicitly supernatural

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:17.120
elements, and it still would have worked.

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:21.080
Instead, the film builds and builds to a climax
in which Thomasin finally gives in to her

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:25.120
curiosity and asks if Black Phillip, the goat,
is really something more.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:29.780
What follows is a full-on Satanic embrace,
complete with a witches' sabbath in the woods,

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:34.470
and Thomasin's ecstatic rise as a young woman
who's able to finally, fully be herself.

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:38.960
It's a horror film ending that's both legitimately
frightening and genuinely celebratory.

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:42.750
Night of the Living Dead remains revolutionary
for a number of reasons.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:48.580
Director George A. Romero's 1968 film created
the modern zombie genre as we now know it,

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:52.650
reshaped our expectations for horror cinema,
and added a layer of social commentary that

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:57.130
was helped along by the presence of Duane
Jones, a black leading actor at a time when

00:08:57.130 --> 00:08:59.680
that was nearly unheard of, as Ben.

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:02.970
Ben spends much of the film arguing with other
white characters about the best course of

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:06.780
action to take, and then manages to emerge
as the sole survivor of the battle at the

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:08.230
farmhouse overnight.

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:11.670
With the sun rising at last, he hears an approaching
rescue crew and stumbles to the window to

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:15.020
catch their attention… only for, well…
this to happen:

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:20.510
"Alright, hit him in the head - right between
the eyes."

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:22.730
"Good shot."

00:09:22.730 --> 00:09:26.740
Yup - he's immediately shot in the head and
thrown onto a pile of burning corpses like

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:27.740
just another ghoul.

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:31.260
It's a shocking twist in the midst of what's
supposed to be a calm resolution - something

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:35.150
many brilliant horror films would take a cue
from in the decades that followed.

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:37.550
It's also a bold statement on the cultural
climate.

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:41.130
For all his will and for all his survival
instincts, Ben was dismissed by people who

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:45.920
were supposed to be his saviors, and became
another casualty of a society eating its own.

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:51.170
The closing credits featuring the body disposal
only make everything that much more stomach-turning.

